[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — browser app only. Next fork, and this is the one that decides whether this actually solves your problem.

  [1mThe fork:[0m a browser log is useless to you unless it leaves the user's machine. Right now nothing in this repo has a
  backend — [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m points at [38;5;153mapi.example.com[39m and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m never calls it. So "debug production issues" needs a
  destination, and picking it determines the whole transport half of the design.

  - [1mYour own endpoint[0m — POST batched log records to a URL you configure. Keeps the repo zero-dependency and matches the
    [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m pattern already here. Costs you a receiver to build and operate, plus batching, retry, and size limits
    on the client. Only viable if you have, or will have, somewhere to receive them.
  - [1mA third-party service[0m (Sentry or similar) — fastest path to logs you can actually read; the vendor handles batching,
    retry, source maps, and search. Costs a dependency in a currently zero-dep project, and sends user data to a third
    party. Hardest to reverse in the sense that you'll shape records around their SDK.
  - [1mConsole only, but structured[0m — levels, timestamps, consistent shape, no transport. Cheap and reversible. But logs
    stay in the user's devtools, so it does not let you debug production — only local and staging repro.

  Transport choice is the expensive one to change later; levels and record shape are cheap either way.

  [1mMy recommendation: your own endpoint[0m — if you have somewhere to receive them. It preserves the zero-dependency
  posture, and a sink is a small enough seam that swapping in a vendor later is contained. If you don't already operate
  a log receiver, don't build one for this — take the third-party option, because a self-hosted sink that nobody stood
  up means logging that ships and never reaches you.
